226: | 1 | 6 | Plan it, note it, ask again. *How do researchers find what they need, keep it honest, and know what to ask next?* | 5 | g6-search-strategy; g6-keep-questioning; g6-quote-paraphrase | AASL.I.D.1 | Connect (key words), wonder, investigate (search, notes), reflect | Planning a search before typing: key terms and synonyms, an exact phrase in quotation marks, which kind of source to try first, then choosing among results by the snippet and the source name (g6-search-strategy; carries IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4, and IV.A.3, owned by g5-choose-sources, grade 5, as review). Note cards that keep paraphrase, quotation (in marks, word for word) and the student's own thinking apart, each with the source and page, and fixing a sentence copied without marks (g6-quote-paraphrase; carries VI.B.2, owned by g2-credit-line, grade 2, as review; begun here, owned later: VI.B.1, owned by g8-fair-use-basics, grade 8, unit 8). When the question is answered, writing the new questions the answer raised and picking one to chase, a question chain in the research log (AASL.I.D.1, owned here: "Keep asking new questions after one answer is found"; carries I.B.2, owned by g4-narrow-and-plan, grade 4, as review). Out: filters and reference works (unit 4); one set citation style (unit 5); databases and operators (grade 9). Readiness check (last ten minutes of day 1): the grade 5 rows in Seams. Days: show-me (hook, the question, the search plan; the question chain modeled on the hook's case), show-me and practice (note cards; the search plan practiced on an invented results page), inquiry (search and note cards on the host question; skill card withdrawn), inquiry (findings shared in groups of four, each student's question chain written and one follow-up picked; self-check), end-of-unit assessment. Skill card: "plan the words, pick by the source, mark the quote, ask the next question". End-of-unit evidence (sitting, about 30 minutes for the class at once, then the unit 2 readiness check): for an unseen question, a search plan and the choice of two of six invented results with a reason (g6-search-strategy); one quotation card and one paraphrase card from a short source with source and page, and one copied sentence fixed (g6-quote-paraphrase); a chain of three follow-up questions that grow from a given finding (g6-keep-questioning). Respond: the opening five minutes of unit 2's day 2 and the mid-year respond day's groups. Tools: the invented results pages, note card templates, the research log (Curio Class writes the results pages, the stand-alone source set and the card). Host: why people settle by rivers, coasts and oases, ss6-mena-settlement in social studies grade 6 unit 3 (days 26 to 38); stand-alone: "How did people keep food from spoiling before refrigerators?" with a closed set of five short invented sources | The band opens with the search plan and honest notes because every later unit searches and notes; the question chain comes with them because it is what an answered search leads to, and it sets up the question every later unit opens with. The search plan model and the citation model arrive from grade 5 here | Candidate: the continuum's grade 6 connect cell on key words from background reading, as the first step of the search plan; its grade 6 notetaking cell (several note forms), as a choice of card layouts; its grade 6 cell on topics of passion within a study, at the question chain | Honest use without shaming (Sensitive ground, item 4): a copied sentence is fixed with the student privately, never shown to the class as an example of a student's work; the practice copy is invented. The social studies host draws only on the physical geography of the region; the sensitivity read checks that the source set shows the region's own sources and voices (C12). The stand-alone case keeps to methods (salting, drying, cold cellars) with no question about food at home |
227: | 2 | 6 | Leave the page. *How can we tell whether a page deserves our trust?* | 5 | g6-lateral-reading; g6-point-of-view; g6-group-wiki | AASL.VI.A.3; AASL.II.A.2; AASL.IV.C.1 | Investigate (judging sources and evidence), construct, express, reflect | Lateral reading as the method for judging a source: leave the page, see what other sources say about its maker and its claim, and go upstream to the original of a statistic, quote or photo, then judge whether the information is accurate, fair in its context and fit for the task (AASL.VI.A.3, owned here: "Judge whether information is accurate, fair in its context and fit for the task"; carries IV.B.3, owned by g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5, as review). The point of view a source takes (word choice, what is left out, who paid for it), weighed before agreeing, in an "I trust this less because..." or "This fits because..." line (AASL.II.A.2, owned here; begun here, owned later: II.B.2, owned by g7-sources-disagree, grade 7, unit 5). Using a group-written online encyclopedia well: how its editing works, its references and edit history on a dated capture or invented copy, and using it as a map to better sources, not the final source (AASL.IV.C.1, owned here). The method, not a checklist of the page's looks (bar part 11, item 3): a polished invented page fails the lateral check and a plain one passes. Out: photos without context, unsourced statistics and machine-made content (unit 4); news (unit 9). Opening retrieval (day 2): fix the item missed in unit 1's sitting. Days: show-me (hook: two invented pages on one claim, one polished and wrong; the question; lateral reading and going upstream), show-me and practice (point of view; three claims checked laterally on "what others say" cards), show-me and practice (the group-written article's references and history; point-of-view lines on two sources), inquiry (judge the host question's sources: a lateral line, a point-of-view line and one article judged for each student's source list; card withdrawn), inquiry (pairs share their judged source lists and the class agrees on the two best sources; self-check). Skill card: "leave the page, ask who is behind it, go upstream, then judge". End-of-unit assessment: the mid-year check (course day 11), which covers this unit in full. Tools: invented pages, "what others say" cards, printed article captures (Curio Class writes them; Source map for the adapted lateral reading lessons). Host: judging the credibility of each source in ela grade 6 unit 3 (days 63 to 86) (e6-short-research), forecasting research in the same weeks; stand-alone: "Can animals or aching knees really forecast rain?" with invented pages on weather sayings | After unit 1 because judging needs sources found by a plan; before social studies uses lateral reading on news in grade 8 and in the same weeks as ELA's credibility work, so ELA's day can reuse this method (the row's either-order line). Point of view and the group-written article sit here because both are judgments of a source made by leaving it or looking behind it | Candidate: the continuum's grade 6 cells on comparing the authority, accuracy (by fact-checking) and point of view of every source, as the source list's judgment lines; its grade 6 cell on whether information is inaccurate or misleading, at the upstream step | Cases are invented or chosen evenly across views; no partisan question; a hoax page is described or invented, never reproduced (Sensitive ground, item 1). The weather sayings are folk knowledge from many places: the case treats them with respect and checks the claim, not the people who hold it. On any open-search inquiry day, the stop rule and a teacher-checked list (item 2) |
228: | 3 | 6 | Build it together. *How can a team turn its research into something that helps people prepare?* | 4 | g6-team-product; g6-make-and-improve | AASL.III.A.2; AASL.III.B.1; AASL.V.B.2 | Construct (with others), express, reflect | A research team picks a fitting way to talk and share its files (a school-provided shared document, a folder, a notebook passed round), says which tool, why it fits and who holds what, and uses it over the unit (AASL.III.B.1, owned here: "Pick a fitting way to talk and share files with a research team"); the team builds an idea together that no member had alone, recording on a team sheet where each part came from (AASL.III.A.2, owned here). Each student chooses what the team's product needs from them (a label set, a one-page explainer, a map, a short spoken explainer) and keeps going through trial and error, recording each version and what changed in a version log (AASL.V.B.2, owned here; carries I.B.3, owned by g3-make-product, grade 3, as review); this uses the try-and-improve habit of science grade 5 (s5-design-test) without criteria, constraints or fair tests, which stay in science. Readiness check (last ten minutes of the mid-year respond day): the grade 5 group and audience rows. Opening retrieval (day 2): the try-and-improve habit from science grade 5, one line on what a test of a version shows. Out: answering readers' comments in a response log (unit 6); a mixed-view team and another class (unit 9). Days: show-me (hook, the question, the team sharing plan and the version log), inquiry (teams research the host question, sharing plan in use, version 1 with the team sheet; card withdrawn), inquiry (version 1 tried on a reader from another team, written teacher feedback, version 2 begun), inquiry (revision finished; the product shared with a younger class or at a display; self-check). Skill card: "share the plan, mark who added what, try it, change it, log it". End-of-unit assessment: the year-end check (course day 17), which scores this unit's sharing plan and version log from the unit's work after the feedback and revision days, and holds the written item on the team's new idea. Tools: the team sheet, the version log, the sharing plan card (Curio Class writes them and the stand-alone source set). Host: hazard warning signs and preparing for them, s6-hazard-forecast in science grade 6 unit 5 (days 116 to 135); stand-alone: "What should a younger class know to get ready for a big storm?" with a closed set of six invented preparedness sources | Last in grade 6 because it gathers the year: a planned search and honest notes (unit 1) and judged sources (unit 2) are what a team brings to build on; the made product and its shared audience close the year's cycle and hand on to grade 7's feedback loop | Candidate: the continuum's grade 6 cells on building on others' ideas and on encouraging team members to share, at the team sheet; its grade 6 express cell on presenting conclusions in several ways, at the product choice; its grade 6 cell on drafting for the audience | Natural hazards (Sensitive ground; sensitivity read): some students have lived through a storm, flood, fire or earthquake, or have family who have. The case is preparing, not disaster stories: no images of injury, and a student may choose a hazard that does not touch them. The teacher page says what the adult does if a student tells of a frightening experience (health's steps, APPROACH.md section field 1). Teams are mixed by the teacher, never by background; only school-provided tools; no student shares contact details |
229: | 4 | 7 | Search smarter, check the hard cases. *How can we find good sources fast and check things that look true?* | 4 | g7-search-results; g7-lateral-hard-cases | None | Connect (background), investigate (search and judging), reflect | Filters (date, kind of source), a reference work for background before the open web, a site's own menus and search box, a subject heading followed to related items, and a choice rule for which three results to open (g7-search-results; carries IV.B.1, owned by g4-search-more-kinds, grade 4, and IV.A.3, owned by g5-choose-sources, grade 5, as review). Lateral reading on harder invented cases: a photo whose context was lost, a statistic quoted without a source, and machine-made text or images (invented or a dated capture), each checked the same way as any source (who made it, what it cites, what others say), and a post shared only after it is checked (g7-lateral-hard-cases; carries VI.A.3, owned by g6-lateral-reading, grade 6, and IV.B.3, owned by g5-first-lateral-check, grade 5, as review). Out: how machine-made text tools work (computer science); judging the use of such tools in one's own work (grade 10). Opening retrieval (day 2): one lateral check on a short invented page (g6-lateral-reading) and one search plan line (g6-search-strategy). Days: show-me (hook: a famous-looking photo with a wrong caption; the question; filters, the reference work and the choice rule), show-me and practice (the hard cases; filtered searches on an invented results page), inquiry (the host question: background from a reference work, a filtered search, and one hard case checked; card withdrawn; findings shared in groups and the self-check in the last ten minutes), end-of-unit assessment. Skill card: "background first, filter, pick three, then check the photo, the number and the maker". End-of-unit evidence (sitting, about 35 minutes): for an unseen question, a filtered search plan with a reference work named and three results chosen by the rule (g7-search-results); an unseen photo, statistic and machine-made paragraph, each checked with what was found upstream and a share-or-not line (g7-lateral-hard-cases). Respond: the opening five minutes of unit 5's day 2 and the mid-year respond day's groups. Tools: invented results pages with filters, printed reference entries, the hard-case set (Curio Class writes them; Source map for the adapted photo and claim lessons). Host: the Dust Bowl feature and film, e7-central-ideas-develop in ela grade 7 unit 2 (days 31 to 56): a 1930s photo checked for its context; stand-alone: "How much water does it really take to make one cotton T-shirt?", a much-repeated number traced upstream | First in grade 7 because the year's comparing (unit 5) and contributing (unit 6) both need fast background and a checked set; the hard cases build straight on grade 6's method a year later, before ELA and social studies lean on it | Candidate: the continuum's grade 7 cells on fact-checking information on websites and social media, at the share-or-not line; its grade 7 visual literacy cell on drawing meaning from a photo or chart, at the photo case; its grade 7 cell on using online encyclopedias and directories for background | Machine-made content (Sensitive ground, item 7): no claim that any tool is reliable or unreliable; the check is the same as for any source; no student uses a tool or needs an account. If the ELA host is used, the photo shows hardship on the 1930s Plains: the case asks about the photo's context and maker, never mocks the people in it; the sensitivity read checks it against the ELA unit's own sources |
230: | 5 | 7 | When sources disagree. *Who should get the credit, when sources tell the story differently?* | 5 | g7-sources-disagree; g7-evidence-discussion; g7-citation-style | AASL.II.B.2; AASL.II.C.1 | Investigate (comparing sources), construct (conclusions), express (discussion, citation), reflect | Comparing how three or more sources with different views treat one question, in a table (the claim, the evidence, the maker, what each leaves out), then saying what a careful reader concludes (AASL.II.B.2, owned here: "Compare how several sources with different views treat the same question"; begun here, owned later: IV.B.2, owned by g8-multiple-views-gather, grade 8, unit 7). A research discussion where every claim points to a source ("my source, a ... report, says...") and a challenge asks where a claim came from (AASL.II.C.1, owned here). Every source, print, video, image and web, cited in one set style in a works-cited list and pointed to in the text (g7-citation-style; carries VI.B.2, owned by g2-credit-line, grade 2, as review; begun here, owned later: VI.B.1, owned by g8-fair-use-basics, grade 8, unit 8); ELA teaches the format for writing the same year, and this unit applies it across media. Out: the four fair use questions (unit 8). Opening retrieval (day 2): a point-of-view line on a short source (g6-point-of-view). Days: show-me (hook, the question, the comparison table), show-me and practice (the citation style; a table on two short sources), show-me and practice (the sourced discussion as a short fishbowl; citations for five mixed sources), inquiry (the host question's table from three or more sources, each cited; card withdrawn), inquiry (the research discussion in groups of five, each student's sourced contributions marked on the record sheet; conclusions shared; self-check). Skill card: "table the claims, ask where it came from, cite it the same way every time". End-of-unit assessment: the mid-year check (course day 28), which covers this unit's table and citation items; the discussion is scored on day 5 on the record sheet, after its fishbowl practice on day 3. Tools: the comparison table, the works-cited model, the discussion record sheet (Curio Class writes them and the source sets). Host: who gets the credit for beating smallpox, ela grade 7 unit 3 (days 61 to 86) (e7-research-questions); stand-alone: "Who invented the light bulb?" with a closed set of four sources that credit different inventors | After unit 4 because the comparison needs a quick, checked source set; in the weeks of ELA's research and citation unit, so the citation style is applied across media while ELA teaches it for writing (the row's either-order line); and before unit 6, which needs cited sources for the class page | Candidate: the continuum's grade 7 cells on weighing an argument's supporting and refuting evidence and on combining evidence into a conclusion, at the table's last line; its grade 7 cell on recognizing others' opinions and judging their evidence with an open mind, at the discussion | Cases are invented or chosen evenly across views (Sensitive ground, item 1); a school may swap a case and keep the ids met. Credit disputes involve real people: the case weighs sources, never ranks the worth of the people. The discussion keeps to sources; no student is asked to speak for a group. Whose voices: the unit records whose work its source set shows (C12) |
231: | 6 | 7 | Our class page. *How could our school waste less, and how do we make our page better with criticism?* | 5 | g7-class-wiki; g7-feedback-loop; g7-group-solutions | AASL.IV.C.2; AASL.III.C.1; AASL.V.D.3; AASL.V.C.3 | Construct (with others), express, reflect | Adding to a class group-written page (a paper wall or a closed class tool): a section with its sources, credit for others' edits it builds on, and the history of changes read (AASL.IV.C.2, owned here: "Add to a shared, group-written site with credit for others' work"). A group using its findings to come up with fresh answers to a shared, neutral school problem, listing ideas before judging them and tying each kept idea to a finding (AASL.V.C.3, owned here; carries III.A.2, owned by g6-team-product, grade 6, as review). Asking readers for comments on named parts of the page and the solution list, answering each comment in a response log (keep, change, or why not), and using a critical comment to improve the work (AASL.III.C.1 and AASL.V.D.3, owned here; carries I.C.3, owned by g4-revise-from-advice, grade 4, as review); the check scores the use of criticism, never a mood. Out: a team mixed by view and another class (unit 9). Opening retrieval (day 2): one citation in the set style (g7-citation-style, unit 5). Days: show-me (hook, the question, ideas before judging, each tied to a finding), show-me and practice (the class page: a section, sources, credit and history), show-me and inquiry (the response log shown in ten minutes; groups research, draft their sections and solution lists, and ask for comments; card withdrawn), inquiry (every comment answered in the log, written teacher feedback, the page and list revised), inquiry (the page and the best-supported ideas shared with the school office or a younger class; self-check). Skill card: "list ideas before judging, tie each to a finding, credit the edit, answer every comment". End-of-unit assessment: the year-end check (course day 35), which scores the revised page section and the response log from the unit's work and holds the written item on an idea and its finding. Tools: the paper class page (or a closed class tool), section templates, the response log (Curio Class writes them and the stand-alone data set). Host: designing a way to reduce a human impact, s7-human-impact-design in science grade 7 unit 6 (days 129 to 158); stand-alone: "How could our school cafeteria waste less food?" with an invented school-wide waste data set and four short invented sources | Last in grade 7 because it gathers the year's checked, compared and cited sources (units 4 and 5) into a page others read, and the feedback loop needs a product to answer comments on; it hands on to grade 8's team product | Candidate: the continuum's grade 7 cell on curating and sharing good sources with classmates, at the class page; its grade 7 cell on criteria for judging a product made with classmates and the teacher, at the response log; its grade 7 cell on sharing research to raise awareness of a problem | Food and meals (Sensitive ground, item 8): no student is asked what they eat, whether they get school meals or what they waste; data are the invented school-wide set or whole-school counts the school already shares, never individual. Some students face food insecurity: the case is about the system (portions, menus, timing), never about who eats what. The class page holds first names or initials only; no public site |
232: | 7 | 8 | Test what you expect. *Should our school start later, and what do people who see it differently know?* | 4 | g8-test-assumptions; g8-multiple-views-gather | AASL.V.A.2; AASL.IV.B.2 | Connect (prior knowledge), wonder (a working answer), investigate (several views), construct, reflect | Before searching, writing what one expects to find; during research, marking each finding that tests that belief, and saying what one now thinks and why, with a line on how one's own first view shaped what one looked for (AASL.V.A.2, owned here: "Catch and test your own assumptions and mistaken ideas while researching"; carries I.D.1, owned by g6-keep-questioning, grade 6, as review). Planning searches on purpose to gather sources that see the question differently (an expert body, people affected, a critic) and recording in the search plan which view each brings (AASL.IV.B.2, owned here; carries IV.A.3, owned by g5-choose-sources, grade 5, as review). Out: stating each view at its strongest in a product (grade 9, g9-fair-sides); a "where I start" note on one's own lens (grade 10). Opening retrieval (day 2): the comparison table's last line on a short three-source set (g7-sources-disagree) and one filter choice (g7-search-results). Days: show-me (hook, the question, the written expectation and the marked findings), show-me and practice (the search plan for views; findings marked on a short set), inquiry (the host or stand-alone question: expectation, a plan for three views, findings marked and an honest update; card withdrawn; updates shared in groups and the self-check in the last ten minutes), end-of-unit assessment. Skill card: "write what you expect, plan for other views, mark what tests it, say what you think now". End-of-unit evidence (sitting, about 35 minutes): for an unseen question, an expectation, a search plan that names three views and the source kind for each (g8-multiple-views-gather); given six findings, those that test the expectation marked, and an honest update with its reason (g8-test-assumptions). Respond: the opening five minutes of unit 8's day 2 and the mid-year respond day's groups. Tools: the expectation card, the views search plan, the invented source sets (Curio Class writes them). Host: how an author answers conflicting viewpoints, e8-conflicting-viewpoints in ela grade 8 unit 2 (days 31 to 62); stand-alone: "Should our school start later in the morning?" with a closed set of six invented sources (a sleep researcher, a bus planner, a family, a coach, a teacher, a student) | First in grade 8 because the year's research product starts from an honest expectation and a plan for other views; it builds on grade 7's comparing (unit 5) a year later and hands the views plan to unit 9 | Candidate: the continuum's grade 8 cell on questioning prior knowledge to find misconceptions, at the expectation; its grade 8 cell on predicting answers and the perspectives likely to be found, at the search plan; its grade 8 cell on changing one's opinion when others' reasons have merit, at the update | Cases are invented or chosen evenly across views (Sensitive ground, item 1). Sleep touches health: the case uses invented sources and asks no student about their own sleep or home routine. The update is scored for following the evidence, never for which side a student ends on |
233: | 8 | 8 | Compare and use fairly. *Why do sites that many people write disagree, and when may we use someone else's work?* | 4 | g8-compare-wikis; g8-fair-use-basics | AASL.IV.C.3; AASL.VI.B.1 | Investigate (comparing group-written sites), construct, express (making with others' work), reflect | With classmates, comparing what several group-written sites say on one question, listing the differences and tracing each back to the references behind it (AASL.IV.C.3, owned here: "Compare with classmates what several group-written sites say on one question"). The basics of copyright and fair use: a work is protected from the moment it is made; fair use weighs four questions (why the work is used, what kind of work it is, how much is used, whether the use takes the place of the original), and giving credit alone does not make a use fair; for invented cases students say which way each question leans and pick the safer path (a public domain or openly licensed work, a smaller part, a link instead of a copy, or asking permission) (AASL.VI.B.1, owned here; carries VI.A.2, owned by g4-honest-use, grade 4, as review). Out: reading licenses and writing a permission request (grade 9, g9-open-licenses); fair use on hard cases such as a parody or a remix (grade 9). Students weigh the questions and never rule a use permitted or lawful. Opening retrieval (day 2): one works-cited entry for an image (g7-citation-style) and one class page credit line (g7-class-wiki). Days: show-me (hook: two group-written entries that give different numbers; the question; the comparison chart traced to references), show-me and practice (the four questions on three invented uses; one more comparison), inquiry (the host or stand-alone question: pairs compare three entries and make one product page with an image chosen by the safer path and credited; card withdrawn), inquiry (pages finished, shared in groups; self-check). Skill card: "line them up, trace each difference, ask the four questions, take the safer path". End-of-unit assessment: the mid-year check (course day 45), which covers this unit in full. Tools: printed captures of invented group-written entries with references, the four-questions card, invented works for the fair use cases (Curio Class writes them). Host: the 1906 food and medicine research, e8-self-generated-research in ela grade 8 unit 3 (days 63 to 88), where period cartoons and photos are public domain and a modern image is not; stand-alone: "How tall is the tallest tree, and who measured it?" with three invented entries that differ | After unit 7 because comparing sites is the views plan turned on group-written sources; fair use sits here, before unit 9's product uses others' images and clips, and the year after the set citation style (unit 5), so students already credit and now learn credit is not enough | Candidate: the continuum's grade 8 cell on making ethical decisions about creating and sharing, remixing and using images within fair use, at the safer path; its grade 8 cell on citing what is specific to a source rather than common knowledge; its grade 8 cell on discussing copyright in safe use of technology | Copyright and fair use (Sensitive ground, item 6): general guidance, never legal advice; no claim that a use is lawful; the `rights` read applies to these days. Every case work is invented or public domain. Whose voices: the unit records whose work its source set shows (C12) |
