# Open library and research skills material for Curio Class: is there a base text we may adapt?

Read 2026-11-16 (subject pre-work, the library counterpart of P2). Research notes, not legal advice. Each licence was
read on the publisher's own page that day unless marked "search only" (seen in a search summary, page not opened).
The bar is docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md: CC BY 4.0, public domain or the UK Open Government Licence v3.0 is a base; NC,
SA, ND or unclear is reference only.

## The short answer

- **A real partial base for grades 6 to 12:** 47 Civic Online Reasoning lessons and assessments (Digital Inquiry Group,
  formerly the Stanford History Education Group) are CC BY 4.0, among them the lateral reading lessons the bar's
  criterion 3 rests on. The rest of that curriculum is CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 and stays reference.
- **A second partial base for grades 9 to 12 and teacher background:** Mike Caulfield, Web Literacy for Student
  Fact-Checkers (2017), CC BY 4.0. Its examples and tools are dated.
- **Nothing for K to 5.** No openly licensed elementary library or information literacy curriculum was found. The
  Empire State continuum (no licence stated) is the grade check set, not a base.
- **Verdict:** K to 5 our own; 6 to 12 our own with these two partial bases, taken item by item. Adopting them is
  Cory's call.

## Candidates

| Candidate | Licence (as read) | Grades | Fit | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Civic Online Reasoning, CC BY items** (Digital Inquiry Group) | The site's Creative Commons page (opened) lists 47 items under "Attribution 4.0 International", for files reached by PDF or shared-drive link. They include: intro lessons on who is behind the information, what the evidence is and what other sources say (levels 1 and 2); lateral reading versus vertical reading; lateral reading with an encyclopedia site, news stories and fact-checking organisations; click restraint; domain names; sponsored content; news versus opinion; evaluating photos, videos and data; verifying claims on social media; and about 21 parallel assessments. Non-curricular site content is the authors' copyright | 6 to 12 | The direct source of the bar's criterion 3; short lessons with assessments | **Partial base**, CC BY items only. Each item checked for third-party screenshots and named platforms, which are replaced |
| Civic Online Reasoning, other items | CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 (same page): the main assessments, four history lessons, the professional learning sessions | 6 to 12 | | Reference |
| **Web Literacy for Student Fact-Checkers** (Caulfield, 2017) | "licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License", "except where otherwise noted" (Whatcom open textbook copy, opened) | 9 to 12, teacher pages | Four moves: look for previous work, go upstream, read laterally, and a habit of checking one's emotions. Many examples name platforms of 2017 | **Partial base** for teacher background and the high school moves; examples replaced |
| Empire State Information Fluency Continuum (Stripling, 2019) | No licence or copyright line on the pages or in the 3 to 5 band file (opened) | PK to 12 | Grade-by-grade skills and priority skills | Reference: the grade check set (STANDARD.md section 2) |
| New Jersey State Library, Teaching Information Literacy pilot (grade-band activities, federally funded) | Not stated (search only) | K to 12 | Possible elementary activities | Unclear. Worth one look by a person at the licence |
| Utah elementary library media scope and sequence | Not stated (search only) | K to 5 | A skills list | Reference |
| News literacy programs (national nonprofits) | Free with accounts, all rights reserved (from memory) | 6 to 12 | | Reference |

## Traps

- The Civic Online Reasoning page says the licences cover files reached by PDF or shared-drive link; anything else
  on the site is the authors' copyright. Credit names the group only in the credit line; no claim that it reviewed
  or endorsed Curio Class.
- Lessons built on live web pages go stale. Every adapted task uses a page we describe or a dated capture made our
  own, with a note for the teacher to swap in a current case.
- The evidence for lateral reading is one district field study in high school (Journal of Educational Psychology,
  2022, from memory, not re-read) and earlier classroom pilots. No page may claim more.

