focused questions generated (W.7.7), in groups that set goals and deadlines for their research and check progress at the end of each week (SL.7.1b, used again); print and digital sources searched with good terms, credibility and accuracy judged (the tonic advertisement is the one to catch), quoted and paraphrased without plagiarism, cited in a standard format (W.7.8). Written as an article planned for task, purpose and audience (W.7.4), with transitions that create cohesion (W.7.2c, used again); drafted in a shared document where partners comment and the writer responds and revises, and published with live links to its sources (W.7.6: produce, publish, link to and cite sources, interact and collaborate; on a shared classroom device or a lab session, no student account). A talk on the findings with salient points in a focused, coherent way, pertinent facts and examples, eye contact and clear speech (SL.7.4), multimedia and a visual display that clarify claims and findings and emphasize salient points (SL.7.5), rehearsed as an informal explanation to a partner and then given formally, language and delivery adapted between the two (SL.7.6) | Research comes before social studies grade 7's Greek research (its unit 9, from day 101), so ELA models the general routine first. Comparing authors on one topic needs the central-ideas work of unit 2. The roots of medical words begin the Latin and Greek roots social studies uses in its Latin legacy unit (about day 134). Retrieval tasks: two accounts of one event (e6-compare-accounts), a grade 6 research question refocused and a basic source list (e6-short-research), a short talk in logical order (e6-present-claims), a typed page with peer comments (e6-produce-publish), technical words and roots (e6-technical-words, e6-word-strategies) | Candidate: research taught and then practiced on more than one source (RUBRIC-ELA 2s, 2t); the word list met again in a later unit (2k, 2l) | Smallpox killed many; stated plainly, no images of the sick. Onesimus was enslaved; Mather owned him; stated plainly. The unit credits Onesimus by name and does not dwell on his enslavement beyond what the credit question needs; the sensitivity agent reads the unit after the study the brief names. Native nations' losses to smallpox are stated as fact, not as a lesson in blame. No student is asked about vaccines in their own family; the CDC pages are the facts used. The tonic advertisement is labeled as written for the course |
| 4 | Athens: who could speak? "How did Pericles want the world to see Athens, and who did his picture leave out?" | 22 | e7-author-position; e7-argument-writing; e7-text-vs-film | RI.7.6; W.7.1; RL.7.7; RI.7.7 | Texts: anchors (rights: our own): an original feature on the Athenian assembly and who counted as a citizen; an original historical story of a potter's son at his first assembly, with a course-made audio version and a course-made short film of one scene (deliberate camera, lighting and sound choices; the paper path gives stills and a shot list); two original editorials on choosing a youth council, one of them also course-recorded as a speech at a town meeting. Paired primary excerpts, short and read with full support (public domain): Pericles' Funeral Oration in Thucydides (Benjamin Jowett's translation, 1881); Aristotle's Athenian Constitution (F. G. Kenyon's translation, 1891); Theodore Roosevelt, "The Right of the People to Rule" (1912), the sound recording (fixed before 1923) with its transcript, optional | The authors' points of view and purposes, and how each sets their position apart from others (RI.7.6): taught on the two editorials and the feature, then applied to Pericles praising Athens against the feature showing who was left out. The story read, then heard in its audio version and watched in its filmed scene: what each medium's techniques (sound, voices and pace; lighting, color, camera focus and angle) do (RL.7.7); the recorded editorial read and then heard: how delivery changes the words' impact (RI.7.7), with the 1912 recording as an optional second example. A short argument in one sitting first, on Pericles' claim. Then an argument through the full process, "Should our town choose its youth council by lot, as Athens chose most officials?": claim introduced, others acknowledged, reasons organised (W.7.1a), relevant evidence from accurate sources (W.7.1b), words, phrases and clauses that create cohesion and clarify the relationships among claims, reasons and evidence (W.7.1c), formal style (W.7.1d), a conclusion (W.7.1e); typed, about two pages. The story is also read against the feature: what it kept and invented (RL.7.9, used again) | Beside social studies grade 7 unit 8 (days 87 to 100 plus held day 120), which asks whether Athens was a democracy; ELA reads how one speech made the case, and its opening feature teaches the assembly and citizenship before the story or the excerpts need them. Argument comes before social studies unit 12 (about day 145), which asks for it. Author's position after unit 3, where authors' shaping was taught; the year's seminar on Pericles uses SL.7.1c's elaboration questions again. Retrieval tasks: author's purpose and a traced argument (e6-author-purpose, e6-trace-argument), the grade 6 argument plan (e6-argument-writing), reading against hearing and watching (e6-read-vs-watch) | Candidate: the argument rubric used all year (RUBRIC-ELA 2p); a speaking look-for (2j) | Athens excluded women, enslaved people and foreigners from the assembly; stated plainly. The youth-council question is about a town's choice, not a school election, and no student is asked to campaign. The 1912 speech is a campaign speech; students are told so |

