| 1 | The missing letter. "What did the missing letter say, and why did someone want it to stay lost?" | 30 | e8-strongest-evidence; e8-theme-relationship; e8-dialogue-incidents; e8-dramatic-irony; e8-reading-range; e8-text-response-writing; e8-collab-discussion; e8-informative-writing; e8-revise-purpose-audience; e8-verbals-voice-mood | RL.8.1; RI.8.1; RL.8.2; RL.8.3; RL.8.6; RL.8.10; RI.8.10; W.8.9; W.8.10; SL.8.1; W.8.2; W.8.5; L.8.3; L.8.1 | Texts: an original Curio Class mystery novella read whole (about 35,000 to 45,000 words, the extra pages read in the home reading; lengthened 2026-11-14 so the band's whole texts climb past grade 5's): two eighth graders volunteering at their town's historical society find that a letter from 1868 has gone missing from its file, and the reader learns more than they do (rights: our own). Paired: Library of Congress and National Archives pages on how archives keep and date letters (public domain, federal works). Independent reading: a long book of the student's choice from the Reading Room shelf or home, logged | The strongest evidence for a point stated and one inferred, and why it beats the others (RL.8.1), and from the archive pages (RI.8.1). A theme and how it grows through characters, setting and plot, with an objective summary (RL.8.2); the line of dialogue or the incident that turns the plot, shows a character or forces a choice (RL.8.3); the suspense and humour that come from what the reader knows and the characters do not (RL.8.6). The discussion routine: prepared (SL.8.1a), rules for collegial discussions and decision-making followed, progress tracked toward specific goals and deadlines, and individual roles defined as needed (SL.8.1b), questions that connect several speakers' ideas (SL.8.1c), views qualified by evidence (SL.8.1d). The evidence paragraph daily (W.8.9a; W.8.9b on the archive pages), short and long (W.8.10). The band and the log (RL.8.10, RI.8.10). An analytical essay on how the author builds suspense, through the full process: the topic introduced clearly, previewing what is to follow, ideas organised into broader categories, with formatting, graphics and multimedia where useful (a timeline of what the reader knows against what the characters know) (W.8.2a), relevant, well-chosen facts, quotations and examples (W.8.2b), appropriate and varied transitions that create cohesion and clarify the relationships among ideas (W.8.2c), precise words (W.8.2d), formal style (W.8.2e), a conclusion (W.8.2f); revised for purpose and audience (W.8.5). Verbals (L.8.1a), active and passive voice (L.8.1b), mood (L.8.1c), shifts fixed (L.8.1d), and voice and mood chosen for effect, such as passive voice to hide who took the letter (L.8.3a). A one-sitting narrative, a review of grade 7 narrative writing used as a retrieval sample: the chapter the letter's writer would have told; feedback addresses grade 7 skills, and grade 8's narrative demands are taught and assessed in unit 4 | A long shared book first, so strongest evidence, dramatic irony and the discussion routine are taught on one text everyone has read. The letter's history opens thread 1 (records and what they keep) the month before unit 2 reads founding documents. Retrieval tasks at first relevant use: several pieces of evidence and theme across a text (e7-cite-several, e7-theme-development, e7-story-elements-interact), two narrators contrasted on the model (e7-contrast-pov), the band, the evidence paragraph and discussion goals (e7-reading-range, e7-text-response-writing, e7-collab-discussion), an informative paragraph and sentence types (e7-informative-writing, e7-revise-purpose-audience, e7-phrases-clauses), the narrative sample (e7-narrative-writing) | Candidate: the novella's text record (RUBRIC-ELA 2b); the discussion look-for sheet (2i); an end task joining reading and writing on the theme (2v) | The letter's contents touch on a family's loss in the Civil War, without battle scenes; no violence in the present-day plot. Some students live apart from a parent; no task asks students about their own family, and the narrative prompt offers a setting outside the home. The novella gets the text agent's read and the sensitivity read |
| 2 | Founding words, answered. "When the founders wrote that all men are created equal, who answered them, and what did they say?" | 32 | e8-word-choice-info; e8-delineate-argument; e8-conflicting-viewpoints; e8-conflicting-texts; e8-argument-writing; e8-pause-omission | RI.8.4; L.8.6; RI.8.8; SL.8.3; RI.8.6; RI.8.9; W.8.1; L.8.2 | Texts: anchors (rights: our own unless named): two original features, one on Benjamin Banneker and his almanac, one on the 1793 yellow fever in Philadelphia and the Free African Society's volunteers; two original editorials on what the town's marker for the 1793 volunteers should say, written to answer each other, one with irrelevant evidence; two short original news reports of the same town meeting that disagree on a fact and on its meaning; an original recorded talk at that meeting. Paired background: National Archives pages on the Declaration and Constitution (public domain, federal works). Paired primary excerpts, short and read with full support (all public domain): the Declaration's second paragraph and grievances; the Constitution's Preamble; Banneker's letter to Jefferson (1791) and Jefferson's reply; Mathew Carey, A Short Account of the Malignant Fever (1793); Absalom Jones and Richard Allen, A Narrative of the Proceedings of the Black People (1794) | Word meaning and the effect of word choice, including analogies and allusions, on tone (RI.8.4): taught on the features and editorials, then applied to the Declaration, read for its word choice ("self-evident") and for who answered it, with Banneker's letter, and its allusion to the Declaration, as the answer; the unit's academic words (L.8.6). An argument delineated and judged, with irrelevant evidence named (RI.8.8): taught on the two editorials, then applied to Banneker's letter, and the same on the recorded talk (SL.8.3). How an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting evidence or viewpoints (RI.8.6): the two editorials first, then Banneker and Jefferson each answering the other. Two texts that disagree on facts and on interpretation (RI.8.9): the two news reports first, then Carey set beside Jones and Allen. A short argument in one sitting first, on Carey's account. Then an argument through the full process, "Our town is putting up a marker for the 1793 volunteers: what should it say?": claim, with counterclaims named and answered (W.8.1a), relevant evidence from the sources (W.8.1b), words that link claims, counterclaims and reasons (W.8.1c), formal style (W.8.1d), a conclusion (W.8.1e); typed, about three pages. Commas, ellipsis and the dash for a pause or break (L.8.2a; a dash appears only in exercise sentences and the texts), the ellipsis to mark words left out of a quotation (L.8.2b), spelling (L.8.2c) | The month after civics 8 unit 2 (the Declaration, days 16 to 30), so the document is not first met here and never in the same month; civics reads it for its ideas, ELA for word choice and response. The Declaration is read again in ELA grade 10 (the liberty set) and grade 11 (its reasoning) and grade 9 reads seminal documents for rhetoric; grade 8 reads it in an excerpt. Argument with counterclaims comes before civics unit 6's court research (about day 77). Point of view against other views comes well before civics unit 9 (about day 131). 32 days: the year's hardest paired texts and its process argument. Retrieval tasks: word choice and tone in an article (e7-word-choice-info), an author's position and a judged argument (e7-author-position, e7-evaluate-argument), two authors on one topic (e7-authors-shape), the grade 7 argument plan (e7-argument-writing), commas between coordinate adjectives (e7-commas-spelling) | Candidate: the 80 percent count of text-dependent questions (RUBRIC-ELA 2g); the argument rubric used all year, with a model piece (2p) | Slavery and the Declaration's exclusions stated plainly; Jefferson enslaved people while answering Banneker. Carey's charge against the Black volunteers and their reply are read as history, with no role play and no student asked to argue Carey's side. Thousands died of the fever. The unit (days 31 to 62) avoids civics unit 6's weeks on Dred Scott and secession (days 77 to 95), so the hardest content in the two courses does not stack |
| 3 | Poison and promises: the 1906 fight over food and medicine. "How did writers and scientists push the country to make a food and drug law in 1906?" | 26 | e8-self-generated-research; e8-produce-publish; e8-central-idea-supporting; e8-word-strategies | W.8.7; W.8.8; W.8.4; W.8.6; RI.8.2; L.8.4; L.8.5b | Texts: Food and Drug Administration history pages on Harvey Wiley, the "Poison Squad" trials and the 1906 Pure Food and Drug Act (public domain, federal works); an original long feature on how the law was made. Paired excerpts (public domain): Wiley's Department of Agriculture bulletins (from 1904), Samuel Hopkins Adams, "The Great American Fraud" (1905), Upton Sinclair, The Jungle (1906), one short meat-packing passage, and the 1906 Act's text. A fenced research set of about fifteen federal pages and short public-domain excerpts (the older items supporting, not anchors) | A central idea in the feature and how supporting ideas carry it, with an objective summary (RI.8.2). Words worked out by context (L.8.4a), new Greek and Latin parts tox, pharm, serv, ced/cede and ad-, taught across days 66 to 73 with weekly returns (L.8.4b); grade 7 demos, logy, pan, epi and immun retrieved, not retaught, general and specialized reference materials, print and digital, used to find a word's pronunciation, precise meaning or part of speech: a dictionary, a glossary of 1906 food and drug terms and a thesaurus (L.8.4c), and checking (L.8.4d); word relationships to sharpen meaning (L.8.5b). A research project on a question the student writes from the unit's texts (who was harmed, what the law left out, what changed after), refined as sources answer and raise questions (W.8.7), in groups that make decisions together, set goals and deadlines and track progress each week (SL.8.1b, used again); print and digital sources searched with good terms, credibility and accuracy judged, quoted and paraphrased without plagiarism, cited in a standard format (W.8.8). Written as an article for a stated audience (W.8.4) with a preview, headings and one chart or table from the 1906 sources (W.8.2a, used again); drafted in a shared document where partners comment and the writer responds and revises, and published with links, the relationships among ideas shown in a chart or timeline (W.8.6: produce, publish, present relationships efficiently, interact and collaborate; on a shared classroom device or a lab session, no student account). Published by about day 84, then a short findings talk with a visual (SL.8.4 and SL.8.5, the first of the year's talks, grade 7's salient points in use) | Thread 2's grade 8 topic begins in history, where science met the press and the law. The research routine is taught in the unit's first two weeks (days 63 to 76), before civics unit 6 (about day 77) uses it. Publishing is held to about day 84 so ELA publishes before civics begins research on day 85. Retrieval tasks: grade 7 research with further questions and a linked source list (e7-research-questions, e7-produce-publish), two central ideas (e7-central-ideas-develop), roots and affixes (e7-word-strategies) | Candidate: research taught and then practiced on more than one source (RUBRIC-ELA 2s, 2t); the word list met again in a later unit (2k, 2l) | The Jungle's factory passage includes injury and spoiled meat; chosen short. Immigrant workers are named with respect. Sinclair argued for socialism; stated as fact. The "Poison Squad" were young men who agreed to eat preservatives; no student tests any food or substance |
| 5 | Gene stories: judging science claims in the news. "When a headline says a gene was found or edited, how do we know what to believe?" | 28 | e8-medium-choice; e8-paragraph-structure; e8-connections-distinctions; e8-present-reasoning | RI.8.7; SL.8.2; RI.8.5; RI.8.3; SL.8.4; SL.8.5; SL.8.6 | Texts: National Institutes of Health MedlinePlus Genetics and National Human Genome Research Institute pages on genes, traits and gene editing; Food and Drug Administration pages on foods from engineered plants (public domain, federal works). One science story told three ways for the course: an original news article, a short recorded explainer and an infographic; two course-written advertisements for products that claim gene-based benefits (no real brands); an original feature on how a newsroom checks a science claim. Paired: the First Amendment (1791, public domain) | How a key paragraph's sentences build its point (RI.8.5). The connections and distinctions the texts draw among genes, traits and environment, by comparison, analogy and category (RI.8.3). A second short research project (W.8.7 and W.8.8, used again), the year's last, on a question the student generates about one gene claim in the news: several avenues opened, sources from a fenced set of federal pages judged for credibility and accuracy, notes quoted or paraphrased, cited in the standard format, and a further question; its findings feed a short informative explanation in one sitting (W.8.2, used again) and the talk. From about day 145: the same science story in print, video and infographic, what each medium does well and badly (RI.8.7), and the purpose behind each source, including the advertisers' commercial motive and what each product costs against what it promises, a light personal finance touch inside the day (SL.8.2). One unseen literary text at the top of the band read without support and scored apart (RL.8.10, independently; the informational one is in check 3). A formal argument talk, "Should our state require labels on gene-edited food?", given to another class in the school, with families invited where the school allows: claims and findings in focused order with relevant evidence and sound reasoning, eye contact and clear speech (SL.8.4), media that clarify and add interest (SL.8.5), formal English adapted to the setting (SL.8.6), with its written text (W.8.1, used again) | Closes thread 2 beside science grade 8 unit 5 (genetics, days 105 to 131), late in the year; ELA's gene pages define the words for a school that runs science later. The research and structure work comes first (days 129 to 144); medium choice and source motive come from about day 145, after civics unit 9 (the press, days 131 to 144); if civics runs later, the newsroom feature teaches the source-checking routine itself. The year's discussion and argument work arrive in a talk to a real audience as civics unit 10's civic action runs (about days 149 to 160). 28 days: four rows, with the second research cycle kept short. Retrieval tasks: how ideas interact and a text's organisation (e7-interactions, e7-text-organization), a talk with salient points (e7-present-salient), two central ideas across media (e7-central-ideas-develop) | Candidate: a cold-text end-of-unit assessment joining reading and writing (RUBRIC-ELA 2v); the year's count of informational and literary anchors (2a) | No student is asked about their own family's traits, health or genes. Gene-linked conditions are named with respect. The history of eugenics is not taught here; if a student raises it, the teacher names it as a wrong done with false science and points to the social studies course. ELA judges the argument and the medium, not the science |

CORE UNITS science-grade-5
| 6 | Protecting water by design. "How can a community keep its water clean, and how do we know our filter works?" Anchor: after a storm, brown runoff pours from a parking lot into a stream that a town downstream drinks from | 18 | s5-protect-resources; s5-design-test | 5-ESS3-1; 3-5-ETS1-3 | Practices: obtaining, evaluating and communicating information (protecting resources); planning and carrying out investigations (fair tests of a prototype). Crosscutting: systems and system models | Information combined about how communities use science ideas to protect Earth's resources and environment: protecting watersheds, cleaning up runoff, restoring wetlands, cutting air pollution, saving water; each action tied to the Earth system it protects; the fresh water case from unit 5. Then the band's full design cycle from that problem: a filter that cleans muddy water (the named pick; a rain catcher that slows runoff is the second choice), with solutions generated and compared, then fair tests of the prototype planned and run with one variable changed and the rest held the same, several trials each, data graphed (a line plot of repeated measurements) before a change is made, and the failure points that show what to improve. Nature of science: repeated trials, because one result can mislead | Needs unit 5's fresh water case: the row supplies the design problem for the design test (the rows' order). Last because it completes the band's engineering standard with everything grades 3 to 5 built, and it runs beside ELA grade 5's water unit | Candidate: a short talk to a younger class on the filter's results (a product, not an action campaign) | Filtered water is never drunk; gloves for muddy or runoff water; wash hands. Communities named for good work and for problems on backed sources only; no student asked about home water; no cause pushed (values rule b). Clarity readings use a printed scale of cloudiness, not chemical tests. Simulation to list: a filter test tool for classes without materials. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

CORE UNITS science-grade-6
| 5 | Natural hazards. Where do natural hazards happen and how do we prepare for them? Anchor: the 2011 earthquake and tsunami in Japan. | 20 | s6-hazard-forecast; s6-digital-signals | MS-ESS3-2; MS-PS4-3 | Practices: analyzing and interpreting data; using mathematics and computational thinking; engaging in argument from evidence (competing designs); obtaining, evaluating and communicating information. Crosscutting: cause and effect; stability and change | Students map the locations, magnitudes and frequencies of natural hazards; they explain earthquake, eruption and earthquake-generated tsunami patterns with the plate model, and storm patterns with unit 3's atmosphere and ocean models; which hazards give warning signs that allow forecasts; which technologies reduce harm (sea walls, warning buoys, evacuation routes, building design); criteria and constraints for a tsunami-safety solution and two options compared (starts MS-ETS1-1 and MS-ETS1-2); how warning systems moved from sirens and analog radio to digital alerts, and why digital signals are more reliable (MS-PS4-3: base lesson 8 and about one more lesson for the signals comparison; wave readiness is checked on day 115 and receives day 117 response). Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.7 (hazard maps and records); WHST.6-8.1 (designs argued against criteria); WHST.6-8.8 (information gathered from several sources) | Fifth in the base; uses unit 4's plate model and unit 3's weather models. Its design start hands on to grade 7 units 5 and 6. ELA grade 6's hazards unit (its unit 6, days 137 to 156) comes after this unit and leans on it (see Cross-subject). | Candidate: none beyond the expectations (settled at zero added days in the scope and sequence). | Loss of life discussed with care: data and design, no graphic images; some students may have lived through a disaster, so the teacher gives a way to step out (the sensitivity agent reads the note after studying the sources in the brief). Histograms: frequency, equal-width intervals and how a histogram differs from a bar graph taught with supplied counts (math 6 teaches them later; about 1 day). Water in a tray as a wave tank, spills wiped. No-kit: public buoy and earthquake records. Simulation listed: a tsunami wave tank. ELA grade 6 unit 6 (days 137 to 156) reads the Galveston 1900 hurricane, a different disaster: coordinate the non-graphic content boundaries and step-out route. Sensitivity read. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |

CORE UNITS science-grade-7
| 6 | Earth's resources and human impact. How do changes in Earth's system impact our communities, and what can we do about it? Anchor: floods and droughts happening more often in communities that used to be steady. | 30 | s7-resource-distribution; s7-climate-change-evidence; s7-population-consumption; s7-human-impact-design | MS-ESS3-1; MS-ESS3-5; MS-ESS3-4; MS-ESS3-3 | Practices: asking questions and defining problems; using mathematics and computational thinking; obtaining, evaluating and communicating information. Crosscutting: stability and change; cause and effect | Warming air and water raise evaporation and change rain; temperature records, carbon dioxide and methane levels and human activity data, questioned to separate correlation from cause: human activities, chiefly burning fossil fuels, are the main cause of the rise in global temperature over the past century, and natural factors are weighed and found smaller (MS-ESS3-5); minerals, fossil fuels and groundwater found where past geologic processes put them, most non-renewable in a lifetime (MS-ESS3-1): base lesson 10 with its extension taught in full (fossil fuel formation and distribution), plus a Curio Class addition of about 1 day on mineral ores and groundwater from maps and geologic evidence, explained with grade 6's rock-cycle and plate models; students then explain an unfamiliar resource distribution from that evidence; population and use per person raising effects on Earth systems unless technologies and choices change (MS-ESS3-4); a method designed to monitor and reduce one human impact on water locally, judged against criteria and constraints (completes MS-ESS3-3, started in unit 5; uses MS-ETS1-2); the resilience plan (base lesson 17) runs in three base days, not four. Also met (literacy, no added days): RST.6-8.1 (evidence from climate and resource readings); WHST.6-8.7 and WHST.6-8.8 (the short research behind the impact plan); WHST.6-8.1 (a cause claim judged against data) | Last in the bas