tten fairly), inquiry (groups gather each view's best evidence on the decision; card withdrawn), inquiry (the memo drafted, images and quotes used as their licenses allow, a permission request drafted where needed), inquiry (written feedback from the teacher and one other group; the memo revised), inquiry (the memo presented to its audience, a school group the teacher arranges; each student writes how the findings changed their first idea; the self-check). Skill card: "strongest case for each side, best evidence, then our choice; read the license before you use it." End-of-unit evidence: the year-end check (day 17) is this unit's end-of-unit assessment, with the memo's change line scored as its III.A.3 item (Course-level checks). Tools: invented case cards for each view, a license sort set of six works with their labels, two fair use case cards, the memo frame, the permission request frame (Curio Class writes all). Host: comparing interpretations, wh1-sources-and-interpretations in social studies grade 9 unit 3 (days 31 to 46); the group talk of ela grade 9 unit 1 (days 1 to 22); stand-alone: "Should our school add water refill stations, and where?" | After unit 1 because fair sides need the judged, logged sources unit 1 taught, and the memo needs a question held over weeks; the license work sits where a product first uses other people's images and quotes. Grade 8 fair use basics lead straight into the depth here | Candidate: the 9-10 bold on retrieving and using images, sound and video by their legal and ethical terms (open-license and public domain image sources, named generically); the 9-10 bold on working with others to research a real problem and propose a change | Cases even across views; the school problem is neutral and no partisan stand is asked. Fair use and licenses are general guidance, never legal advice; students weigh the questions and never rule a use lawful (APPROACH.md item 6, `rights` read). Honest use without shaming: a student who used a work wr