### 1. Unit 2 does not teach its owned accuracy standard in full

**Severity:** Major  
**LAYER:** standard, progression, frame  
**REPEAT:** new  
**Confidence:** firm

**Location:** Frame, Devices bullet and Unit 2, line 176; `g5-first-lateral-check`; `aasl-words.txt`, AASL.IV.B.3.

**Problem:** The frame treats finding a page�?Ts maker and purpose through an external card as complete evidence for AASL.IV.B.3. The actual competency requires systematic assessment of information�?Ts validity and accuracy. The own-words gloss has narrowed that requirement, and the progression and assessment follow the narrower gloss. [AASL learner framework, second edition](https://standards.aasl.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/250918-AASL-framework-for-learners.pdf).

Unit 2�?Ts accuracy and verification work remains merely a �?oCandidate�?? in Check set adds. Unit 3 later marks factual differences without requiring students to resolve them.

**Why it matters:** A student could identify a credible maker and an informative purpose, accept an incorrect statement, and still pass the owned standard.

**Exact fix:**

1. Replace the AASL.IV.B.3 gloss with an own-words phrase that includes checking information, for example:

   > Check important information against other evidence before deciding whether to use a source.

2. Retain Unit 2�?Ts maker-and-purpose lateral check, then require students to test one important factual statement against supplied independent evidence and mark it **supported, contradicted or unresolved**, with a reason.
3. Put that complete sequence in the show-me, practice, inquiry and unseen assessment cases. Include a credible-looking source containing an incorrect statement.
4. Update `g5-first-lateral-check`, its skill card, the Devices claim and the Check set read account accordingly.

Keep grade 6�?Ts additional upstream tracing and contextual evaluation. No standard needs to move between grades.

