3 Smallpox: who gets the credit? "Who should get the credit for beating smallpox, and how do authors decide?" e7-authors-shape; e7-research-questions; e7-present-salient; e7-word-choice-info; e7-word-strategies; e7-produce-publish Smallpox killed many; stated plainly, no images of the sick. Onesimus was enslaved; Mather owned him; stated plainly. The unit credits Onesimus by name and does not dwell on his enslavement beyond what the credit question needs; the sensitivity agent reads the unit after the study the brief names. Native nations' losses to smallpox are stated as fact, not as a lesson in blame. No student is asked about vaccines in their own family; the CDC pages are the facts used. The tonic advertisement is labeled as written for the course
4 Athens: who could speak? "How did Pericles want the world to see Athens, and who did his picture leave out?" e7-author-position; e7-argument-writing; e7-text-vs-film Athens excluded women, enslaved people and foreigners from the assembly; stated plainly. The youth-council question is about a town's choice, not a school election, and no student is asked to campaign. The 1912 speech is a campaign speech; students are told so
