3 Poison and promises: the 1906 fight over food and medicine. "How did writers and scientists push the country to make a food and drug law in 1906?" e8-self-generated-research; e8-produce-publish; e8-central-idea-supporting; e8-word-strategies The Jungle's factory passage includes injury and spoiled meat; chosen short. Immigrant workers are named with respect. Sinclair argued for socialism; stated as fact. The "Poison Squad" were young men who agreed to eat preservatives; no student tests any food or substance
4 Myths made new. "Why do writers keep retelling Persephone and Nasreddin Hodja, and what makes a retelling new?" e8-myth-made-new; e8-word-choice-lit; e8-compare-structures; e8-narrative-writing; e8-production-fidelity Hades takes Persephone: the retellings keep it to a seizing and a bargain, with no romance. Nasreddin Hodja tales are read as a living tradition shared across Turkey, Iran, Central Asia and beyond, not as quaint, and get the sensitivity agent's read after it studies the tradition's own sources (brief). The class stages scenes; no student plays a person being harmed

